+++ b/README.md
@@ -20,3 +20,21 @@ followed by `0` or `1`), or when it does not resolve: a missing member, an array
 range, has leading zeros or is `-` (the nonexistent element), or a step through a primitive or `null`.
 Only own properties are followed, so keys such as `__proto__` or `constructor` never reach the
 prototype chain. The document is not mutated.
+
+## JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from "./src/index.js";
+
+applyPatch({ foo: "bar" }, [{ op: "add", path: "/baz", value: "qux" }]); // { foo: "bar", baz: "qux" }
+applyPatch({ a: [1, 2] }, [{ op: "move", from: "/a/0", path: "/a/1" }]); // { a: [2, 1] }
+```
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies an array of operations (`add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`
+and `test`) in order and returns the resulting document as a new value; the input document and the patch
+are not mutated and the result shares no structure with them. Paths are JSON Pointers (RFC 6901), with
+`-` addressing the end of an array for `add`. `test` compares with JSON equality (object member order is
+ignored). It throws an `Error` when the patch is invalid (not an array, an operation that is not an
+object, an unknown `op`, a missing or mistyped `op`, `path`, `from` or `value`), or when an operation fails
+(a missing target, an out-of-range index, a failed `test`, a `move` into its own child); no partial result
+is returned. Keys such as `__proto__` are plain members and never reach the prototype chain.
